'''ascribe''' (a̤s-krīb′), ''v. t.''; pret. and pp. ''ascribed'', ppr. ''ascribing''. [Altered to ''ascribe'' (after L.) from early mod. E. ''ascrive'', < ME. ''ascriven'', < OF. ''ascrire'' (''ascriv-'') = It. ''ascrivere'', < L. ''ascribere'', annex by writing, add to a writing, enroll, enter in a list, impute, attribute, < ''ad'', to, + ''scribere'', write: see ''scribe''.] '''1†.''' To add in writing; append (one’s name) to a document, etc.; subscribe.
{{smaller block|The ''ascribing'' of my name would . . . have substracted from . . . the weight of those discourses.
{{right|''Nethersole'', Self-Cond., p. 3. (''N. E. D.'')}}}}
'''2†.''' To inscribe or dedicate.
{{smaller block|The secound pillor called Dorica, being ''ascribed'' to Hercules.
{{right|''Shute'', Archit., C ij. b. (''N. E. D.'')}}}}
'''3†.''' To enroll or register.
{{smaller block|He would long since have been ''ascribed'' a member there.
{{right|''Aubrey'', in Letters of Emin. Pers. (Bliss), II. 632. (''N. E. D.'')}}}}
'''4.''' To attribute, impute, or refer, as to a cause or source; assign; set down: as, losses are often to be ''ascribed'' to imprudence.
{{smaller block|This Speech is, I think, the finest that is ''ascribed'' to Satan in the whole Poem.
{{right|''Addison'', Spectator, No. 321.}}

But many atrocious proceedings must, doubtless, be ''ascribed'' to heated imagination, to perverted principle, to a distaste for what was vulgar in morals, and a passion for what was startling and dubious.
{{right|''Macaulay'', On History.}}}}
'''5.''' To attribute, as a quality or an appurtenance; consider or allege to belong.
{{smaller block|I . . . will ''ascribe'' righteousness to my Maker.
{{right|Job xxxvi. 3.}}

They have ''ascribed'' unto David ten thousands, and to me they have ''ascribed'' but thousands.
{{right|1 Sam. xviii. 8.}}}}

