'''ascript†''' (as′kript), ''a.'' [< L. ''ascriptus, adscriptus'', pp. of ''ascribere, adscribere'', annex by writing: see ''ascribe, adscript''.] Registered; enrolled.

'''ascription''' (ạs-krip′shọn), ''n.'' [< L. ''ascriptio(n-)'', an addition in writing, lit. the act of ascribing, < ''ascribere'', pp. ''ascriptus'', add to a writing: see ''ascribe''.] '''1.''' The act of ascribing, imputing, or affirming to belong, to be due, etc.
{{smaller block|Self-abnegations often repeated imply on the part of the actor a tacit ''ascription'' of relative selfishness to others who profit by the self-abnegations.
{{right|''H. Spencer'', Data of Ethics, § 96.}}}}
'''2.''' An expression ascribing; words in which one ascribes.
{{smaller block|Offering up the ''ascriptions'' justly due to Him for such singular deliverances and blessings.
{{right|''Lincoln'', in Raymond, p. 391.}}}}
Also rarely ''adscription.''

'''ascriptitious''' (as-krip-tish′us), ''a.'' [< L. ''ascripticius'', enrolled as a citizen, soldier, etc. (''ascripticii servi'', slaves bound to the soil), < ''ascriptus'', pp. of ''ascribere'', enroll: see ''ascribe, ascript''.] '''1.''' Bound or attached to the soil: applied to villeins under the feudal system, who were annexed to the freehold and transferable with it. —'''2.''' Added, as to a list; enrolled.
{{smaller block|An ''ascriptitious'' and supernumerary god.
{{right|''Farindon'', Sermons, p. 82.}}}}

