'''aseity''' (a-sē′i-ti), ''n.'' [= F. ''aséité'', < ML. ''aseitas'', the state of being of one’s self, independent existence, < L. ''a se'', of one’s self: ''a'' for ''ab'', of, from; ''se'', self: see ''se''.] The mode of being of that which is underived from anything else; independent existence; existence by self-origination.

{{smaller block|By what mysterious light have you discovered that ''aseity'' is entail’d on matter?
{{right|''Gentleman Instructed'' (ed. 1732), p. 425.}}}}

{{smaller block|The absolute being and ''aseity'' of God. {{right|''W. R. Smith.''}}}}

'''Aselli''', ''n.'' Plural of ''Asellus'', 1.

'''asellid''' (a-sel′id), ''n.'' An isopod of the family ''Asellidæ''.

'''Asellidæ''' (a-sel′i-dē), ''n. pl.'' [NL., < ''Asellus'' + ''-idæ''.] A family of isopodous crustaceans, of which the genus ''Asellus'' is the type. {{smaller|It also contains the genus ''Limnoria'', of which the species ''L. terebrans'', the gribble, is destructive to submerged wood. Other genera are ''Iæra'' and ''Munna''. Its various forms inhabit both fresh and salt water.}}

'''Asellota''' (as-e-lō′tä̤), ''n. pl.'' [NL., < ''Asellus'' + ''-ota''.] A synonym of ''Asellidæ''.

'''Asellus''' (a-sel′us), ''n.'' [L., dim. of ''asinus'', an ass: see ''ass''<sup>1</sup>.] '''1.''' [Pl. ''Aselli'' (-ī).] A name given to each of the two stars γ and δ Cancri, lying east of the quadrangle of that constellation.—'''2.''' [NL.] The typical genus of the family ''Asellidæ''. ''A. aquaticus'', the water hog-louse, is a common form in fresh water.

